(docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #115 - the list-element landing site (the register's
 own examples). Fixing the general cast surfaced a second, latent bug:
 `infer_expr_type` answered a hardcoded Integer for ANY element read off
 an inline list literal, heterogeneous or not, so the cast's own tag load
 overwrote the real per-slot tag `generate_expr` had already left in r11
 with that wrong guess. Element 1 (a genuine integer) happened to cast
 correctly by coincidence; element 2 (a text) was cast as though it were
 the integer the wrong guess claimed, and its raw pointer passed straight
 through - into a `text` destination (SIGSEGV) and a `number` destination
 (prints the address) alike. A heterogeneous literal's element read is
 now answered as runtime-tagged, matching the named-list case
 `infer_expr_type` already handled, so the destination decides the cast
 and "two" correctly fails to parse as a number rather than leaking its
 address.)

a text called t is element 1 of [1, "two", 3].
Print "{t}".
a number called n is element 2 of [1, "two", 3].
On error Print "error flag set".
Print "n={n}".
